@X0BSetting up the control panel-
-------------------------------
 
@X03First, create an OS/2 icon on your desktop.  An easy way to do this is to open
your @X02OS/2 SYSTEM @X03folder.  Once inside, open your @X02COMMAND PROMPTS@X03 folder.  Now,
right click on the @X02OS/2 WINDOW @X03icon.  Click on @X02CREATE ANOTHER @X03line (NOT the
arrow).  Change the title to CONTROL PANEL, then click on CREATE.  The SETTINGS
screen pops up.  AT this point,there is a control panel icon in your command
prompts folder.  Do not close your SETTINGS window yet.
 
The SETTINGS screen should like like the following:

    @X07Path and filename to execute:
    ------------------------------
    @X0F\pcb\pcbcp.exe
@X07
    Optional parameters:
    ------------------------------
    @X0F\pcb\main\usernet.xxx

    @X07Working directory:
    ------------------------------
    @X0F\pcb

@X03Close the SETTINGS window by double-clicking on the square icon in the
upper-left corner of the window.  It will save automatically.

Drag the Control Panel icon from the command prompts folder to your @X02STARTUP
@X03folder (which is also under OS/2 SYSTEM).  Now, the control panel will launch
automatically when you load OS/2.
 
@X0BConfiguring The Control Panel-
---------------------------------
@X03Load the control panel by double-clicking on the control panel icon.  (Dragging
a shadow of the icon to the launchpad makes things easier!)
 
Select @X02FILE@X03 and go to node limits.  Select your low node number.  This is the
first node that the control panel can control.  This does *not* tell control
panel to launch the node. It only allows it to control the node.  Once it's
selected, do the same for the high node number.  This is the last node that
control panel can control.
 
Select @X02OPTIONS@X03.  Click on @X02SAVE...@X03 .  This will save the high and low node
numbers.  Now select @X02FILE@X03 again and go to node settings.  From here, select the
first node that you'd like the control panel to *LAUNCH*.

The Filename to Execute field should be the name of the BAT file or CMD file to
execute.  The working directory should be \PCB (although you should have a
directory under that for each node).  Optional parameters should be left blank.
@X02Optional Port Number @X03should be set to the port number that the node will be
accessing.  Remeber that port 0 or COM0 starts local node and doesn't use a
modem.
 
Click on @X02RUN SETTINGS @X03and select your preference for the appearance of the
nodes.  At first, you may want to select windowed so that you can tell what
they're doing and see that they're coming up.

Set your @X02AUTO RUN SETTINGS @X03to RUN ON STARTUP.  This should launch the nodes
when the control panel is run.  Go back to @X02NODE SETTINGS @X03and make sure that
the information entered is still there.  If not, re-enter it.

If you need batch files for your control panel, they can be downloaded from
@X0ESalt Air BBS@X03.  The filename is @X0CCMD.ZIP@X03.  You'll need to set the port #, node #,
drive letter, data path, and current directory.  The rest will be set up for
you.



 
 




















 
